I’m avoiding floating-point opacity entirely: the implementation computes half-even rounding with integers, then calls the supplied `bin` primitive. For positive `s = n + m`, `(s + (s // 2) % 2) // 2` equals Python’s `round(s / 2)` on both parity cases, including `.5` ties. This keeps the unbounded input theorem in integer arithmetic.
